# Line endings.
#
# Every text file in this project is stored and checked out with Unix endings, so
# a file's bytes do not depend on which machine last touched it. That is not
# cosmetic here: tools/geometry_version.py fingerprints geometry sources by
# reading them in binary mode, so a file that arrives as CRLF on one machine and
# LF on another produces two different digests for identical geometry, and every
# part built from it re-renders for no reason.
#
# `eol=lf` governs checkout, `text=auto` governs commit -- so a CRLF file written
# by an editor or by a tool is normalized on the way into the index rather than
# being caught later by a reader.
#
# core.autocrlf is false on this machine, which means nothing here is being
# undone by a global setting. These rules travel with the repository and do not
# depend on that staying true.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Exports from tools we do not control, and do not edit by hand.
#
# OpenVSP writes CRLF; OpenSCAD and FreeCAD write whatever the platform hands
# them. Normalizing these would rewrite close to a million lines across the
# meshes alone, and the next export would put it straight back. They are read by
# machine, never opened in an editor, and their diffs are not meaningful to a
# person, so git is told to treat them as opaque and leave the bytes alone.
*.stl   -text
*.obj   -text
*.step  -text
*.csg   -text
*.vsp3  -text

# Binary. `text=auto` would detect these from their NUL bytes anyway; naming them
# means the classification is a decision rather than a heuristic.
*.jpg    binary
*.png    binary
*.blend  binary
